[h1]Better Commerce Screen UI by Najane[/h1]

The Commerce screen is where you decide what every settlement in your empire lives on - and the game makes you do it one drag at a time, with the numbers you need scattered across two columns and a scrollbar.

This mod turns it into something you can actually work in. [b]Nothing about the rules changes[/b] - everything it does, you could do by hand, it just takes a great deal longer.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]What you get[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]The mouse does more[/b] - right-click an assigned resource to send it back to the pool, and hold [b]Shift[/b] to do it to every resource of that kind at once. Shift while assigning fills a settlement instead of placing one.
[*][b]Assign All, Reassign All, Unassign All[/b] above the tabs, plus a priority picker and quick assign on every settlement card.
[*][b]Optional automation[/b] - one setting decides what happens when a resource arrives while the screen is closed. Set to [b]Never[/b] until you change it.
[*][b]Trade routes at a glance[/b] - origin, destination and domain on one title line, three cards to a row, and a running total of the routes you could still open.
[*][b]Empire resources in numbers[/b] - not "+1 Gold in all settlements" but what that is actually worth to your empire.
[*][b]A new Factory Resources tab[/b] for the Modern age, showing what your slotted resources produce and what the idle ones would add.
[*][b]Treasure convoys tidied[/b] - homeland settlements dropped from the "not generating" list, each card down to the two numbers that matter.
[*][b]A calmer screen[/b] - icon tabs, no standing instruction paragraph, shorter filter boxes, and no "resources can be assigned" nag while every settlement is full.
[/list]

A [b]?[/b] beside the buttons spells the mouse shortcuts out in game, since none of them can be found by looking.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]How Assign All decides[/h2]

Unhappiness first: any settlement below zero Happiness is fed until it is back at zero, cities before towns, and the help is spread rather than dumped - three settlements at -10, -20 and -30 are levelled out instead of one being rescued while the others stay in the red. After that come resources whose situational bonus actually applies there, then single-yield resources, then multi-yield ones, and last the ones that only make units cheaper.

What a resource is worth somewhere is read from the game's own data, [b]conditions included[/b] - a bonus that needs a city, your capital or a particular building is not counted where it would not happen.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Automation[/h2]

One setting under [b]Options - Mods[/b], for when a resource arrives while the screen is closed:

[code]Never                        the mod does nothing on its own
Place the new resource       just the one that arrived, nothing else touched
Place everything unassigned  the arrival is a cue to tidy up the whole pool
Rebuild every assignment     clears every settlement and starts again[/code]

Anything short of the last option never moves a resource you have already placed, and never touches one you deliberately left out.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Empire resources[/h2]

The Empire tab told you what each resource does - "+1 Gold and Happiness in all settlements" - which reads the same whether you hold one settlement or twenty. It now tells you what that is worth to you:

[code](icon) GOLD [6]
One:  +12 (gold)  +12 (happiness)
All:  +72 (gold)  +72 (happiness)[/code]

Above the tabs, a single income total for the whole empire. Combat bonuses name every unit class they reach, including the ones the game's own description leaves out, and bonuses that are capped or only pay during a Celebration say so instead of quietly inflating the total.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Factory resources[/h2]

A Modern-age resource pays nothing for being owned - it only does anything once it sits in a settlement with a Factory, and the game has no screen for them. The new tab has two halves: what is [b]in factories[/b] and the real total it produces, and what is [b]not assigned[/b] and exactly what it would add the moment you place it. One line above the tabs totals the GDP they earn towards the Modern economic legacy path.

An optional [b]Factories first[/b] checkbox places them the way the game rewards: a settlement runs only one kind at a time but any number of copies, so each factory is started on the kind you have most of and filled with it.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Trade routes and treasure convoys[/h2]

A route card used to carry its destination in a sentence and the other leader's gold at the bottom. Now the title line says all of it and three cards fit in a row. One line above the tabs says how many routes you are running and how many you could, with a tooltip naming every leader you still have room for - split into the ones you could sign [b]right now[/b] and the ones where nothing of theirs is [b]in range[/b]. The unavailable routes are grouped the same way.

Treasure convoys get the same three-to-a-row layout, homeland settlements are dropped from the "not generating" list, and each card shows the [b]Gold[/b] and [b]GDP[/b] it pays instead of a sentence about them.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Compatibility[/h2]

Saved games are unaffected and [b]no game rules, values or balance are changed[/b]. Safe to add or remove mid-game.

⚠️ [b]Not compatible with Resource+.[/b] Both replace the same screen; run one or the other.

[hr][/hr]
